# Research brief

Source: `world/experiment-repo` issue #1, “Research objective”, read 2026-10-17. The issue supplies no attachments, datasets, or additional related material.

## Objective (verbatim)

> Optimizer provenance fact sheet (a small look-up task).
>
> Produce a one-page fact sheet on three optimizers used in deep learning: Adam, AdamW (decoupled weight decay) and Lion (EvoLved Sign Momentum). For each optimizer give the paper that introduced it: its title, its first author, the year it first appeared, and its arXiv identifier.
>
> Every fact must be looked up on the web during this task, not given from memory, and each fact must cite the URL of the page it was read from. Find each paper with a web search first (do not start from a URL you already know), then open the page the search found to read the facts. If your environment has no web search tool, say so in your record and find the page another way the environment offers.
>
> Division of work: experiment-repo looks up Adam and records what it found, with its search query and URLs, in its repository; paper-repo looks up AdamW, collects the three entries and writes the fact sheet (`fact-sheet.md`) as the delivery; internal-review-repo independently looks up Lion and checks each entry of the fact sheet against a source it opens itself, reporting any disagreement before it approves.

## Interpretation and evidence requirements

The supplied division settles ownership: experiment-repo supplies Adam provenance; paper-repo supplies AdamW provenance and the one-page fact sheet; internal-review-repo supplies Lion provenance and independently verifies all three entries. The first-appearance year should be supported by the earliest submission date, distinguished from a later conference or revision date. Each of the four requested fields needs a source URL, and discovery must precede opening the paper page. No experiments, figures, or quantitative comparisons are requested.

No dedicated web-search tool is available in this session. The available research-network resource permits HTTPS access to arXiv and scholarly metadata services; use their search endpoints for discovery and explicitly record that fallback, its query, and the page opened. No optimizer bibliographic facts have yet been established at brief creation.

## Established Adam provenance

The completed task-time lookup is recorded in `adam-provenance.md`, with saved search results and the opened source in `evidence/`. The source <https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.6980>, discovered through arXiv web search, gives *Adam: A Method for Stochastic Optimization*, first author Diederik P. Kingma, first submission 22 December 2014, and arXiv:1412.6980. The first-appearance year is therefore 2014, distinct from the page's conference-publication year of 2015. AdamW and Lion remain their assigned owners' lookups.
